---
// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT OR Apache-2.0
// SPDX-FileCopyrightText: 2026 The Viv authors
// How to connect an agent to the MCP endpoint (server/mcp, served at /mcp by a
// Netlify Function). Every client's steps and syntax come from the vendors'
// pages as read on 2026-09-24 (server/mcp/README.md); a step those pages did
// not record is marked as such rather than guessed. Every code block gets the layout's copy button
// (src/lib/chrome.js); this page keeps the buttons visible and announces a copy.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Code from '../components/Code.astro';
import Toc from '../components/Toc.astro';
import PACK from '../data/promptPack.json';

const URL = 'https://viv-lang.org/mcp';
const TOC: [string, string][] = [
  ['endpoint', 'The endpoint'],
  ['claude-code', 'Claude Code'],
  ['claude', 'Claude'],
  ['cursor', 'Cursor'],
  ['vscode', 'VS Code'],
  ['chatgpt', 'ChatGPT'],
  ['no-mcp', 'Without MCP'],
  ['tools', 'What to ask'],
  ['limits', 'Limits'],
];

const CURSOR = JSON.stringify({ mcpServers: { viv: { url: URL } } }, null, 2);
const VSCODE = JSON.stringify({ servers: { viv: { type: 'http', url: URL } } }, null, 2);

const TOOLS: { name: string; what: string; prompt: string }[] = [
  {
    name: 'search',
    what: 'Searches the published posts, the example programs, the lib/ modules, the builtins and the documented examples.',
    prompt: 'Search Viv for a module that solves a symmetric eigenvalue problem, and show me how to import it.',
  },
  {
    name: 'get_post',
    what: 'Returns a published post: its source, its tests, its lane and the other versions in its family.',
    prompt: 'Get the Viv post runge-phenomenon and explain what its tests check.',
  },
  {
    name: 'viv_reference',
    what: 'Returns the language reference: the grammar, the Python habits that fail, the builtins, the examples by topic.',
    prompt: 'Read the Viv reference, then write a Viv program that integrates sin from 0 to pi with the trapezoid rule and tests the answer.',
  },
];
---

<Base
  title="Use Viv with your agent · Viv"
  description="Connect Claude Code, Claude, Cursor, VS Code or ChatGPT to Viv's MCP server at https://viv-lang.org/mcp: three read-only tools to search the site's programs and modules, read a post and read the language reference. No sign-in."
  eyebrow="Agents"
  heading="Use Viv with your agent">
  <div class="withtoc agents">
    <div>
      <p class="lede">
        Viv has an MCP server. Add one URL to your agent and it can search the site's programs and
        modules, read a post, and read the language reference. Every tool only reads, so there is
        no account and no sign-in. To run a program, paste it into the <a href="/workbench">workbench</a>
        or build Viv from the repository.
      </p>

      <h2 id="endpoint">The endpoint</h2>
      <Code code={URL} />
      <p>
        It speaks the Streamable HTTP transport, revisions 2025-03-26 through 2026-07-28. Most
        clients need only this URL. Pick yours below and copy the line.
      </p>

      <h2 id="claude-code">Claude Code</h2>
      <Code lang="sh" code={`claude mcp add --transport http viv ${URL}`} />
      <p>Then type <code>/mcp</code> in a session to see the server and its tools. <a href="https://code.claude.com/docs/en/mcp">Claude Code's MCP docs</a> cover the rest.</p>

      <h2 id="claude">Claude (claude.ai and Desktop)</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>Open <b>Customize</b>, then <b>Connectors</b>, then <b>Add custom connector</b>.</li>
        <li>Paste the URL and leave the OAuth fields under Advanced settings empty.</li>
      </ol>
      <Code code={URL} />
      <p class="note">
        On Team and Enterprise plans an owner adds the connector in Organization settings. A free
        account gets one custom connector. <a href="https://support.claude.com/en/articles/11175166">Anthropic's help article</a> has the details.
      </p>

      <h2 id="cursor">Cursor</h2>
      <p>Add the server to Cursor's <code>mcp.json</code>:</p>
      <Code lang="json" code={CURSOR} />
      <p class="note">The file is <code>.cursor/mcp.json</code> in a project, or <code>~/.cursor/mcp.json</code> for every project. <a href="https://cursor.com/docs/context/mcp">Cursor's MCP docs</a> have the rest.</p>

      <h2 id="vscode">VS Code (Copilot agent mode)</h2>
      <p>Add the server to VS Code's <code>mcp.json</code>:</p>
      <Code lang="json" code={VSCODE} />
      <p class="note">The file is <code>.vscode/mcp.json</code> in a workspace; the command <b>MCP: Open User Configuration</b> opens the one for every workspace. <a href="https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/copilot/customization/mcp-servers">VS Code's MCP docs</a> have the rest.</p>

      <h2 id="chatgpt">ChatGPT</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>On the web, open the <a href="https://chatgpt.com/plugins">Plugins</a> page, press the plus button and choose <b>Add custom MCP server</b>.</li>
        <li>Give it a name, enter this URL under Connection, choose no authentication, and create it as a plugin.</li>
      </ol>
      <Code code={URL} />
      <p class="note">OpenAI's page on <a href="https://developers.openai.com/apps-sdk/deploy/connect-chatgpt">connecting an MCP server to ChatGPT</a> has these steps and says which account and workspace policies apply.</p>

      <h2 id="no-mcp">Without MCP</h2>
      <p>
        An agent that cannot use MCP can still read the language. The prompt pack is the grammar,
        the Python habits that fail and every documented example with its output, about {PACK.kTokens}k
        tokens. Paste it into the conversation, or give the agent the link:
      </p>
      <Code code="https://viv-lang.org/viv-prompt-pack.md" />
      <p>The full reference adds the command line, the backend matrix and the engines that run each builtin:</p>
      <Code code="https://viv-lang.org/llms-full.txt" />
      <p>
        Both are plain files: <a href="/viv-prompt-pack.md">viv-prompt-pack.md</a> and
        <a href="/llms-full.txt">llms-full.txt</a>. The MCP server serves the same two as the
        resources <code>viv://prompt-pack</code> and <code>viv://llms-full</code>.
      </p>

      <h2 id="tools">What to ask</h2>
      <p>One prompt per tool, to try once the server is connected. The agent picks the tool.</p>
      {
        TOOLS.map((t) => (
          <div class="tool">
            <h3><code>{t.name}</code></h3>
            <p>{t.what}</p>
            <Code code={t.prompt} />
          </div>
        ))
      }

      <h2 id="limits">Limits</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Requests are rate limited per address.</li>
        <li>The posts the tools see are the ones published when the site was last built.</li>
        <li>A solution post is never returned or searched, so a problem's answers stay hidden.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="note">
        The client steps above come from each vendor's documentation as read on 2026-09-24, with Cursor's, VS Code's and ChatGPT's read again on 2026-10-16. The
        server was tested with direct requests for every tool; none of these clients has been tried
        against it yet.
      </p>
      <p class="sr-only" aria-live="polite" id="copy-status"></p>
    </div>
    <Toc items={TOC} />
  </div>

  <style>
    h2 {
      scroll-margin-top: 72px;
    }
    .agents :global(.copy-btn) {
      opacity: 1;
    }
    .agents :global(pre.code) {
      padding-right: 5.5rem;
    }
    .agents ol,
    .agents ul {
      max-width: var(--measure);
      padding-left: 1.3rem;
    }
    .agents li {
      margin: 0.3rem 0;
    }
    .tool h3 {
      margin-bottom: 0.2rem;
    }
    .tool p {
      margin-top: 0.2rem;
    }
  </style>

  <!-- a file, not an inline script, so the Content-Security-Policy needs no new hash -->
  <script is:inline src="/agents-copy.js" defer></script>
</Base>
